   "question": "How does a hash table handle collisions, and what affects its performance?",
   "model_answer": "A hash table maps a key through a hash function to a bucket index. A collision occurs when two keys map to the same bucket. Separate chaining stores colliding entries in a linked list or tree at that bucket; open addressing instead probes for another free slot using linear probing, quadratic probing or double hashing. Performance depends on the quality of the hash function, which should spread keys uniformly, and on the load factor, the ratio of entries to buckets. With a good hash and a low load factor, lookups, inserts and deletes take O(1) on average, but many collisions degrade them towards O(n). Tables therefore resize and rehash when the load factor crosses a threshold such as 0.75.",
