   "question": "How would you use caching to speed up a read-heavy web service, and what problems must you handle?",
   "model_answer": "Put a fast in-memory cache such as Redis in front of the database and use the cache-aside pattern: on a read, check the cache first; on a miss, read from the database, store the result in the cache with a time-to-live, and return it. This cuts database load and latency for hot data. The main problems are invalidation and staleness: when data changes you must update or delete the cached entry, or accept staleness bounded by the TTL. Memory is limited, so an eviction policy such as LRU decides what to drop. You also need to handle cache stampedes, where many requests miss at once after an entry expires, using locking or request coalescing, and measure the hit ratio to see whether the cache is effective.",
