   "id": "Q11", "topic": "Operating Systems",
   "question": "What conditions are required for a deadlock, and how can deadlocks be prevented?",
   "model_answer": "A deadlock occurs when a set of processes each wait for a resource held by another, so none can proceed. Four conditions, the Coffman conditions, must all hold: mutual exclusion (a resource is held by one process at a time), hold and wait (a process holds resources while waiting for more), no preemption (resources cannot be forcibly taken away), and circular wait (a cycle of processes each waiting on the next). Prevention breaks at least one condition, for example by imposing a global ordering on resource acquisition to remove circular wait, requiring processes to request all resources at once to remove hold and wait, or allowing preemption. Alternatives are deadlock avoidance with the Banker's algorithm, or detection and recovery using a wait-for graph.",
