   "question": "What are ACID properties in database transactions?",
   "model_answer": "ACID describes guarantees of a reliable transaction. Atomicity means a transaction is all or nothing: if any step fails the whole transaction is rolled back. Consistency means a transaction moves the database from one valid state to another, preserving constraints such as foreign keys and balances. Isolation means concurrent transactions do not interfere, so the result is as if they ran one after another; databases offer isolation levels such as read committed, repeatable read and serializable that trade strictness for performance and allow anomalies like dirty or phantom reads at weaker levels. Durability means once a transaction commits its changes survive crashes, typically through a write-ahead log. A bank transfer is the classic example: debit and credit must both happen or neither.",
